WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:08.390 align:start position:0%
 
on the 13th  of may  1884  which happens to be just 49 years ago  this week  i was a student at the ohio state  university  president mendenhall a notable teacher  was a professor of physics  in electricity  he had the faculty of making  quite clear and understandable those  fundamental relations of voltage and  current of inductance and capacity  and the like  and the  cgs system of units  after graduating and taking my ab degree  a year later  i remained for a time studying  analytic mechanics and working in the  electrical laboratory  and then i went to johns hopkins for a  year and a half  a little later the  uh threatened receivership led to the  dissolution of the school  and i  looked for a job  i visited my old friend henry umphrey  at the uh  tunnel where he was trying to make 16  candle power incandescent lamps  overcome the  smoke of the locomotives  at the end of the tunnel  he showed me a little shack  with engine and dynamo  which his head was the westinghouse  system  of alternating current  that was new idea to me  he explained the  converter uh we now call them  transformers  uh and i said why yes that's a that's a  room corp coiled backwards isn't it  with a letter of introduction which he  gave me i  met a contractor in philadelphia who was  wiring the bottom local motorworks  after an hour's argument he tried to say  goodbye pleasantly by suggesting that i  work a week for nothing  and they would charge me nothing for the  experience  but after a week of 10 hours a day  i did get a job  at 10 and a half a week  i was helper and wireman  draftsman and foreman  and  ran the dynamo on one of the uh night  ferry boats over to camden then the job  shifted to plainfield  and after a little while it disappeared  i had uh  six months rich experience  and the last month's page still coming  to me  well  during the evenings  i found  william stanley's paper on automating  current aie paper  a regular gold mine of information  i visit pittsburgh  i got lots of inspiration from a meeting  of the national electric light  association  could get no job at westinghouse  so i went on home in ohio  uh conducted a correspondence  which i followed up by a visit to  pittsburgh  and then on the 13th of uh  of august 1888  at six o'clock in the evening i began as  assistant in the testing room  it was before the days of self-harming  bearings and we had to  pour oil in the bearings a little while  and wipe the shaft in  and keep the voltage constant and read  the thermometers  and sweep out the rough wooden floor  all for 11 cents an  hour uh presently i was transferred to  the laboratory where schallenberger had  just invented his meters still well his  regulators and tesla was experimenting  with the induction motors  those were the days of cut and try  methods  and many of the technical mysteries  which battled us are now  taught to  college students  you

